# Mutation case for "a scan keeps what a hazard names".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_scan_keeps_what_a_hazard_names.cases`.
#
# The LSCQ row of SCE Protocol-Synthesis RFC §synth-5-P frees a segment through
# hazard pointers (`queue::hazard`). A hazard is a participant's published claim
# on the object it is about to touch, and a scan frees a retired object only if
# no hazard names it. The case below has the scan ignore the hazards, so a
# segment a participant is in is freed under it.
#
# The oracle is the unit test `a_scan_keeps_a_node_a_hazard_names_and_frees_the_rest`
# in `hazard.rs`. It holds a hazard on one retired node, retires another that
# nothing names, runs a scan, and counts what was freed. It is deterministic
# because the scan reads the slots once and the test holds the slot still across
# it. It is not the loom models of
# `a_segmented_queue_orders_every_segment_hand_over.cases`: that file used to
# carry this case, and no model there could see it (measured 2026-10-10, below).
#
# Why no loom model sees it. The race a hazard closes needs two threads in one
# segment while a third retires it, and the allocator has to make a segment freed
# under a reader a failure, which loom, seeing atomics and cells and not memory,
# does not. A model that did both (consumers poisoning what is freed) failed on
# the unmodified queue, and so did a probe of the hazard handshake written with
# plain loom atomics and `SeqCst` throughout, a protocol that is correct under
# C11: loom 0.7.2 lets a `SeqCst` load read a value the sequential order has
# already replaced, so it reports a race the memory model forbids. A model that
# fails on correct code cannot be an oracle.
#
# What this casefile does NOT cover, and nothing in the tree does yet. The other
# half of the handshake is the order of the publication and the check in
# `Hazard::protect`: a hazard must be published before the location it is
# validated against is read again, which the two `SeqCst` accesses give. Taking
# both to `Relaxed` was run under Miri on the LSCQ real-thread tests with eight
# seeds (the forge-rust gate's own command, 2026-10-10), and every test passed
# with no report. Loom cannot judge it for the reason above, and ThreadSanitizer
# was not run against it. The tool that models `SeqCst` is GenMC, whose harnesses
# so far cover the C11 queues and not the Rust hazard domain. Until a harness
# covers it, that weakening is unobserved; it is not a case here because a case
# no oracle can catch is a permanent SURVIVED, which says nothing.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-forge-runtime --lib queue::hazard::tests
mutation_targets backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/queue/hazard.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/queue/hazard.rs

mutation_case "a scan frees a node a hazard names" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/queue/hazard.rs",
     r'''            if named.contains(&(node as *mut ())) {''',
     r'''            if false && named.contains(&(node as *mut ())) {''')
PY
